  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Resource Development / Compromise Accounts — Cloud Accounts", "technique": "T1586.003", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-12 09:14:08 CEST, Wazuh's Office365/Azure module logged a burst of OAuth authorization-code-flow consent events. data.srcip=185.220.101.47 issued GET /common/oauth2/v2.0/authorize requests carrying client_id values for three never-before-seen third-party applications — 'DocumentReviewerPro-fake1a2b3c', 'MailSyncAssistant-2026-xf9a', 'FileShareHelper-corp-spoof' — each requesting the broad scope set 'Mail.Read Mail.Send Files.ReadWrite.All Contacts.ReadWrite offline_access'. The HTTP Referer was 'https://malicious-app-portal.io/install?promo=free' and Origin 'https://malicious-app-portal.io', consistent with a victim following a phishing link to an attacker-hosted app-install page. Wazuh fired custom rule 91220 ('OAuth app consent granted by user — new external app') and 91221 ('OAuth scope includes Files.ReadWrite.All or Mail.Send'). The app display names deliberately mimic legitimate productivity tools to lower the victim's suspicion at the consent prompt.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'office365' and rule.description matched 'Consent to application' / 'OAuth app consent' with data.srcip=185.220.101.47 and a Referer pointing to an external app-install portal. The univariate signature: a single external source driving GET /authorize for multiple unknown client_id values with offline_access + write scopes inside a 4-minute window.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:14:08+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Credential Access / Steal Application Access Token", "technique": "T1528", "what_happened": "At 09:21:30 CEST (~3 minutes after the consent burst), the same data.srcip=185.220.101.47 issued POST /common/oauth2/v2.0/token requests against the IdP host 10.108.57.184. The first exchange used grant_type=authorization_code with client_id='DocumentReviewerPro-fake1a2b3c' to trade the consent's authorization code for an access token + refresh token. A second request used grant_type=refresh_token with the same client_id to mint a fresh long-lived token — the classic offline_access persistence move. Wazuh fired rule 91225 ('Access token used to enumerate mailbox from new app'). The attacker now holds a bearer token scoped to Mail.Read/Mail.Send/Files.ReadWrite.All/Contacts.ReadWrite for amir.kassab's tenant identity.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=185.220.101.47 POSTed to the /oauth2/v2.0/token endpoint on data.dstip=10.108.57.184 with grant_type=authorization_code then grant_type=refresh_token for the SAME client_id seen in step 1's consent burst. rule.description matched 'OAuth token' / 'access token' and rule.id 91225 fired. The refresh_token exchange establishes offline_access persistence — the token survives the user's password reset.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:21:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Collection / Exfiltration — token-driven mailbox, contact and file access", "technique": "T1114.002", "what_happened": "From 09:26:10 to 09:41:55 CEST, data.srcip=185.220.101.47 used the bearer token to drive a burst of Microsoft Graph-style API calls against 10.108.57.184: GET /api/v1/me/messages and /mailFolders/inbox/messages?search=password and ?search=invoice (Mail.Read enumeration targeting credentials and finance content), GET /api/v1/me/contacts?top=100 (Contacts.ReadWrite harvest), GET /api/v1/me/drive/root/search(q='password'), search(q='vpn'), search(q='credentials') (Files.ReadWrite.All OneDrive/SharePoint search for secrets), and finally POST /api/v1/me/sendMail to address 'exfil-collect@protonmail.com' with body 'Sync complete. 247 contacts, 93 messages exported.' (Mail.Send exfiltration confirmation). Wazuh fired rule 92100 ('Abnormal volume of file API reads from OAuth token'). This is the post-consent data-access stage: the illicit-consent OAuth app is now actively reading mail/contacts/files and exfiltrating a summary to an attacker-controlled mailbox.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=185.220.101.47 generated a sustained sequence of /api/v1/me/* requests (messages, contacts, drive/root/search) on data.dstip=10.108.57.184 immediately after the token exchange in step 2, with rule.id 92100 firing on read volume and a POST /sendMail to an external protonmail address. The targeted search terms (password, invoice, vpn, credentials) and the outbound sendMail to a free-mail provider are the canonical illicit-consent data-collection-and-exfiltration signature.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:26:10+0200"}
  ],
